body:
As I said in our Web Engines Hackfest discussion on this topic, I'm unsure who this really serves: 

1. It's possible for me to build a bundler which loads chunks that can be renamed to the sha1 of their contents. In fact bundlers _effectively_ do this today (take a [look at this Rollup example](https://rollupjs.org/repl/?version=4.62.2&--) and note how it rewrites the import to `'maths-BtnDB6gu.js`). So nothing about the existing module resolution _prevent_ me using hash based naming, but using existing tools gives me more flexibility about the naming arrangement.
2. The kind of secondary feature here, it seems, is to inline declare a lazy module. This is also possible to do today with lazy import maps and data URIs. The ergonomics are not significantly worse than what is described in this new proposal, and I can even use multiple layers to provide a similar content hashing and alias system if I really want to, so the result is something like:
	```html
	<!doctype html>
	<button>Load injected feature</button>

	<script type="module">
	  document.querySelector("button").addEventListener("click", async () => {
	    const mod = await import("injected-feature");
	    mod.run();
	  });
	</script>

	<!-- My hashed data URI -->
	<script type="importmap">
	  {
	    "imports": {
	      "hashed--7df76d0728390dc845f71949343ff476c619743b24701007cfa40c21b56603fe": "data:text/javascript;charset=utf-8,export%20function%20run()%20%7B%0A%20%20console.log(%22Injected%20feature%20module%20loaded%20from%20a%20data%3A%20URL%22)%3B%0A%7D%0A"
	    }
	  }
	</script>

	<!-- My global name map -->
	<script type="importmap">
	  {
	    "imports": {
	      "injected-feature": "data:text/javascript,export * from 'hashed--7df76d0728390dc845f71949343ff476c619743b24701007cfa40c21b56603fe'"
	    }
	  }
	</script>
	```
3. The fact that is possible but not hugely predominant in the ecosystem to me suggests no one is actively seeking this feature, so we may be potentially designing for a set of constraints developers do not have, or said more pointedly: designing a feature that users don't want and haven't asked for.
4. Some of this can get _really_ complicated really fast: if a user has to provide a content hashing system it needs to be incredibly deterministic and identical to the same content hashing system in the browser, otherwise things break. While I was working at GitHub, we saw these kinds of breakages with Subresource Integrity; where the algorithms for determining the SRI might change ever so slightly in one tool to the next, or the precise steps of the bundler might cause some null-byte or line ending which one tool didn't properly hash, and so the SRIs would mismatch causing the site to go down. While at GitHub we _unshipped_ SRI due to this, and instead poured the effort into hardening the CDN infra. This is somewhat analogous to XHTML: providing brittle features that do not have a good error recovery story means failing the user, therefore the developer will stop using them. To ground this back to this functionality:
	```html
	<!-- This is what I author on the page -->
	<style exportblob="sha1:h0TJuPEXp/P6adSyOua/coEhWrw=">
	* { color: red };
	</style>

	<!-- Oops, my minifier tooling came in and mangled the CSS, but didn't update the hash, now I have a broken site -->
	<style exportblob="sha1:h0TJuPEXp/P6adSyOua/coEhWrw=">*{color:red};</style>
	```
6. I'm somewhat skeptical of introducing features which are effectively predicated on the use of build tooling. Obviously no one is going to hand-calculate sha hashes, but this feature is essentially declaring "if you want shared resources in your shadow trees you'll need to use content addressable hash exports" - this mandates accidental/incidental complexity for developers who now need to introduce tooling to use these features. This raises the barrier to entry for A) users who want the underlying feature (shared imports) and B) entrants into the tooling or server side ecosystem, who now inherit this complexity (i.e. new tool X can't be used for user Y until it introduces this feature). This seems really unfortunate.

There _are_ features we could certainly tease out here, though, which I think users would greatly benefit from:
 - A way to inject modules into the module map declaratively using inline `<script>` or `<style>` - users ask for this, and it could be helpful.
 - A way to inject style sheets into the module map declaratively. Users have also asked for this.

> I guess what I was trying to solve is "people want to export global inline styles from within shadow roots, without creating accidental name conflicts".

Yes I think the issue is the bit after the comma is presumed by us, and potentially already resolved for the current working use-cases. I think the actual problem statement for developers is: "want to export global inline styles from within shadow roots, and I can deal with name conflicts easily enough myself".

However, if we want to have some higher degree of enforced encapsulation we already have import map scopes which resolve the issue from the other side - perhaps the concept can be carried over. In case anyone needs a brief primer; let's say library `foo` wants to import `bar`, but `bar` is clobbered by a global map, then we can simply avoid this issue by expressing a scope:

```html
<script type="importmap">
{
  "imports": {
    "bar": "/bar.js",
    "foo": "/vendor/foo/index.js"
  },
  "scopes": {
    "/vendor/foo/": {
      "bar": "/vendor/foo/bar.js"
    }
  }
}
</script>
```

I would suggest that if we are to propose a mechanism for exporting named exports, we should have similar mechanics - resolving the scopes - so `/vendor/foo` can never export a top level `bar` because `bar` is defined in that libraries scopes.
